cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Muqarnas: the vault from its plan"
message: >-
  If you use this software, its reconstruction, or its findings, please cite it
  as below. Software is MIT licensed; the writing, visuals, and data are
  CC BY 4.0.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jameel
    given-names: Muhammad
abstract: >-
  A computational reconstruction of the muqarnas vault implied by the
  thirteenth-century gypsum plate found at Takht-i Sulaymān. The element
  alphabet and all element geometry are generated in exact arithmetic over
  ℚ(√2) on the coordinate module (ℤ + ℤ·√2/2)²; the plate's element
  arrangement is digitized from published vector line work and regularized onto
  that lattice. A tier-assignment solver returns every valid reading of the
  corner-starting family rather than the first, and two of them are raised as
  differing vaults from the same projection, in a scroll-driven WebGPU piece.
  The digitization surfaces a measurable finding: the plate's regular field
  spans 7 + 3.5√2 ≈ 11.9497 modules, and the incised design absorbs the misfit
  to an even 12 by stretching one band by exactly the remainder 5 − 3.5√2.
keywords:
  - muqarnas
  - Islamic architecture
  - Takht-i Sulaymān
  - architectural reconstruction
  - computational geometry
  - exact arithmetic
  - al-Kāshī
  - digital humanities
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
